"OpenAI CVE assignment policy | OpenAI (https://openai.com/policies/openai-cve-assignment-policy/)\nciteturn2423view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2421view1\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 143\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 20, 2025\nL14: # OpenAI CVE assignment policy\nL15: \nL16: Scope\nL17: \nL18:   * cite3†Scope L19:   * cite4†Acknowledgment and communication L20:   * cite5†CVE ID assignment and tracking L21:   * cite6†Researcher recognition and rewards L22: \nL23:   * cite3†Scope L24:   * cite4†Acknowledgment and communication L25:   * cite5†CVE ID assignment and tracking L26:   * cite6†Researcher recognition and rewards L27: OpenAI is a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) for vulnerabilities in our products and services. We assign CVE IDs for security issues reported by external parties in OpenAI systems and publish CVE Records to the cite7†public CVE list⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com . We are committed to a transparent, cooperative disclosure process that keeps our users safe while giving credit to the researchers who help keep our platforms secure.\nL28: ## Scope\nL29: \nL30: This policy covers technical security vulnerabilities discovered outside OpenAI’s standard internal security processes in OpenAI products that could reasonably compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of OpenAI systems, software we distribute, and data.\nL31: AI model safety vulnerabilities which include behavior or content (prompt “jailbreaks,” model hallucinations, policy bypasses, etc) are not within scope of this policy. We encourage reporting AI safety issues through dedicated cite8†channels .\nL32: \nL33: To report a vulnerability in our systems, please see our cite9†Coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy .\nL34: ##\nL35: Acknowledgment and communication\nL36: \nL37: Initial response: We will aim to acknowledge receipt of vulnerability reports within 3 business days.\nL38: \nL39: Assessment and triage: Our security team will investigate the report to validate the vulnerability and determine its impact and severity. During this phase, we may contact you to request additional information.\nL40: \nL41: Updates: We will share information about the progress of the investigation when there is noteworthy information to share, as appropriate.\nL42: Disclosure: While we are working on a fix, we ask that you do not share or publicize the vulnerability details. OpenAI likewise will treat your report as confidential. We won’t disclose your report outside of OpenAI and relevant partners, consistent with our cite10†Outbound Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure policy . Keeping the vulnerability non-public can help protect our users and systems until a solution or sufficient mitigation is in place.\nL43: Once a solution or sufficient mitigation is in place, we will work with you to coordinate a disclosure timeline.\nL44: ## CVE ID assignment and tracking\nL45: \nL46: Assigning a CVE ID helps us and the community track the issue across our communications and ensures it’s recognized in global vulnerability databases.\nL47:   * Scope for assigning a CVE ID: We will generally assign a CVE ID for security vulnerabilities in software that we distribute, including those that require user action to resolve. We will generally not reserve a CVE ID for server-side issues. We will only assign a CVE ID for security vulnerabilities that are exploitable and that are important security issues. We will not assign a CVE ID for defense-in-depth fixes, misconfigurations, nor informational findings.\nL48:   * When we assign the CVE ID: In many cases, we will reserve a CVE ID early in the process, once we’ve confirmed the issue and begun working on a resolution. Once a CVE ID is reserved for the vulnerability, we’ll share that ID with you. The CVE ID remains in a reserved (not yet public) state before public disclosure.\nL49:   * OpenAI disclosure: We will maintain a disclosure page with confirmed, public CVEs on OpenAIs Trust Portal at cite11†trust.openai.com⁠(opens in a new window)†trust.openai.com . Public CVE ID entries will link to related advisories published by OpenAI.\nL50: ## Researcher recognition and rewards\nL51: \nL52: We appreciate the contributions of security researchers.\nL53: \nL54:   * Public acknowledgment: If requested, we will credit the reporter (by name or alias, and/or affiliation) in any published advisory or release notes, as well as in the CVE disclosure page.\nL55: \nL56:   * Bug bounty rewards: If a report was submitted through our bug bounty program, the reporter may be eligible for a monetary reward, subject to our published rules and discretion.\nL57: Thank you for helping keep OpenAI and our users safe. We view the relationship with the security community as a collaboration. By following this policy and working with us in good faith, you’re making a real difference. We’re committed to reciprocating that good faith by being responsive, fair, and appreciative of your efforts. If you have any questions about these guidelines or need clarification on our CNA process, feel free to reach out at disclosure@openai.com.\nL58: Let’s continue to work together to protect and secure the technology that’s shaping our future!\nL59: Research\nL60: \nL61:   * cite12†Research Index L62:   * cite13†Research Overview L63:   * cite14†Economic Research L64: \nL65: Latest Advancements\nL66: \nL67:   * cite15†GPT-6.1 Sol L68:   * cite16†GPT-6 Astra L69:   * cite17†GPT-5.6 L70:   * cite18†GPT-5.5 L71: \nL72: Safety\nL73: \nL74:   * cite19†Safety Approach L75:   * cite20†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L76:   * cite21†Security & Privacy L77:   * cite22†Trust & Transparency L78: \nL79: Products\nL80:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L81:   * cite23†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L82:   * cite24†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L83:   * cite25†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L84:   * cite26†Codex†chatgpt.com L85:   * cite27†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L86:   * cite28†Release Notes L87: \nL88: API Platform\nL89: \nL90:   * cite29†Overview L91:   * cite30†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L92:   * cite31†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L93: \nL94: Business\nL95:   * cite32†Overview L96:   * cite33†Solutions L97:   * cite34†Resources L98:   * cite35†Plugins L99:   * cite36†Customer Stories L100:   * cite37†Partner Network L101:   * cite38†Contact Sales L102: \nL103: Developers\nL104: \nL105:   * cite39†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L106:   * cite40†Open Models L107:   * cite41†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L108:   * cite42†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L109:   * cite43†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L110: \nL111: Company\nL112:   * cite44†About Us L113:   * cite45†Our Charter L114:   * cite46†Careers L115:   * cite47†News L116: \nL117: Support\nL118: \nL119:   * cite48†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L120:   * cite49†OpenAI Status(opens in a new window)†status.openai.com L121: \nL122: More\nL123: \nL124:   * cite50†Stories L125:   * cite51†Academy L126:   * cite52†Supply Co. L127:   * cite53†Livestreams L128:   * cite54†Podcast L129:   * cite55†RSS L130: \nL131: Terms & Policies\nL132: \nL133:   * cite56†Terms of Use L134:   * cite57†Privacy Policy L135:   * cite58†Other Policies L136: cite59†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L137: \nL138: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL139: \nL140: English United States\nL141: \nL142: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing group chats in ChatGPT | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/group-chats-in-chatgpt/)\nciteturn2423view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2421view2\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 13, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # Introducing group chats in ChatGPT\nL17: \nL18: Collaborate with others, and ChatGPT, in the same conversation.\nL19: \nL20: cite2†Try in ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Collaborating together with ChatGPT\nL25: \nL26:   * cite4†Collaborating together with ChatGPT L27:   * cite5†How it works L28:   * cite6†Privacy and control by design L29:   * cite7†What’s next L30: \nL31:   * cite4†Collaborating together with ChatGPT L32:   * cite5†How it works L33:   * cite6†Privacy and control by design L34:   * cite7†What’s next L35: Update on November 20, 2025: Early feedback from the pilot has been positive, so we’re expanding group chats to all logged-in users on ChatGPT Free, Go, Plus and Pro plans globally over the coming days. We will continue refining the experience as more people start using it.\nL36: Today, we’re beginning to pilot a new experience in a few regions that makes it easy for people to collaborate with each other—and with ChatGPT—in the same conversation. With group chats, you can bring friends, family, or coworkers into a shared space to plan, make decisions, or work through ideas together.\nL37: Whether you’re organizing a group dinner or drafting an outline with coworkers, ChatGPT can help. Group chats are separate from your private conversations, and your personal ChatGPT memory is never shared with anyone in the chat.\nL38: Group chats are starting to roll out on mobile and web for logged-in ChatGPT users on ChatGPT Free, Go, Plus and Pro plans in Japan, New Zealand, South Korea and Taiwan. This pilot is a small first step toward shared experiences in ChatGPT, and we expect to learn from early user feedback to inform how we expand to more regions and ChatGPT plans.\nL39: ## Collaborating together with ChatGPT\nL40: \nL41: Group chats make it possible to bring people, and ChatGPT, into the same conversation. For example, if you’re planning a weekend trip with friends, create a group chat so ChatGPT can help compare destinations, build an itinerary, and create a packing list with everyone participating and following along.\nL42: For more hands-on projects, like designing a backyard garden or finding art for a new apartment, create a group chat with your partner or roommates to help collaborate on design ideas, preferences, and styles together. It’s also great for group decisions, like finding a restaurant that fits everyone’s tastes or settling a friendly debate with an impartial referee.\nL43: Group chats also make collaboration at work or school easier. You can draft an outline or research a new topic together. Share articles, notes, and questions, and ChatGPT can help summarize and organize information.\nL44: ## How it works\nL45: To start a group chat tap the people icon in the top right corner of any new or existing chat. When you add someone to an existing chat, ChatGPT creates a copy of your conversation as a new group chat so your original conversation stays separate. You can invite others directly by sharing a link with one to twenty people, and anyone in the group can share that link to bring others in.\nL46: When you join or create your first group chat, you can set up a short profile with your name, username, and photo so everyone knows who’s in the conversation. Group chats can be found in a new clearly-labeled section of the sidebar for easy access.\nL47: Group chats work much like your usual ChatGPT conversations — only now, others can join in. Responses are powered by GPT‑5.1 Auto, which chooses the best model to respond with based on the prompt and the models available to the user that ChatGPT is responding to based on their Free, Go, Plus or Pro plan. Search, image and file upload, image generation, and dictation are enabled.\nL48: Rate limits only apply when ChatGPT responds–not to messages between users in the group chat–and responses from ChatGPT count toward the limit available of the person ChatGPT is responding to. For more details, you can visit the cite8†help center⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com .\nL49: We’ve also taught ChatGPT new social behaviors for group chats. It follows the flow of the conversation and decides when to respond and when to stay quiet based on the context of the group conversation. You can always mention “ChatGPT” in a message when you want it to respond. We’ve also given ChatGPT the ability to react to messages with emojis, and reference profile photos—so it can, for example, use group members’ photos when asked to create fun personalized images within that group conversation.\nL50: You can also manage the group settings—tap on the participants’ icons to name the group, add or remove people, or mute notifications. You can set custom instructions for how ChatGPT responds in each group chat, whether that’s sharing more context or giving specific tone or personality.\nL51: ## Privacy and control by design\nL52: \nL53: Group chats are separate from your private conversations. Your personal ChatGPT memory is not used in group chats, and ChatGPT does not create new memories from these conversations. We’re exploring offering more granular controls in the future so you can choose if and how ChatGPT uses memory with group chats.\nL54: You’re in control. You have to accept an invitation to join a group chat. Everyone can see who’s in the chat or leave at any time. Group members can remove other participants with the exception of the group creator, who can only be removed by leaving themselves.\nL55: Additional safeguards for younger users. If someone under 18 uses group chats, ChatGPT automatically reduces exposure to sensitive content for everyone in the chat. Parents or guardians can turn group chats off entirely through cite9†parental controls⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com .\nL56: ## What’s next\nL57: \nL58: Group chats are just the beginning of ChatGPT becoming a shared space to collaborate and interact with others. As ChatGPT becomes an even better partner in group conversations, it will help you spark ideas, make decisions, and express your creativity with the people who matter most in your life. As we pilot this experience, we’ll learn how people use ChatGPT together and will continue refining based on early feedback.\nL59: \nL60:   * cite10†2025 L61:   * cite11†ChatGPT L62: \nL63: ## Author\nL64: \nL65: OpenAI\nL66: ## Keep reading\nL67: \nL68: cite12†View all L69: \nL70: cite13†A practical guide to building with GPT-6 ProductOct 2, 2026 L71: \nL72: cite14†DevDay 2026 Recap CompanySep 29, 2026 L73: \nL74: cite15†Introducing GPT-6.1 Sol ProductSep 29, 2026 L75: \nL76: Research\nL77: \nL78:   * cite16†Re